      "text": "Given an acute triangle \\(ABC\\) with foot of the perpendicular from \\(C\\) to \\(AB\\) being \\(P\\) and the foot of the perpendicular from \\(B\\) to \\(AC\\) being \\(Q\\), and the line \\(PQ\\) intersects the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) at points \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\). We know \\(XP = 10\\), \\(PQ = 25\\), and \\(QY = 15\\). We need to find the value of \\(AB \\cdot AC\\) in the form \\(m\\sqrt{n}\\) and determine \\(m + n\\).\n\nFirst, we note that \\(PQ\\) is a segment of the altitude corresponding to the right triangle \\(BCQ\\) and its foot \\(P\\) on \\(AB\\). Since \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are feet of perpendiculars, \\(PQ\\) is the same segment as the altitude from \\(C\\) to \\(AB\\). The power of point \\(P\\) with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) can be written as:\n\\[ PA \\cdot PB = PQ^2 = 25^2 = 625. \\]\nThis is because \\(P\\) lies on the circumcircle, and the power of a point \\(P\\) is the product of the segments of the chord it intersects.\n\nNext, we use the fact that \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) are the intersection points of the line \\(PQ\\) with the circumcircle. Since \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) are distinct points on the circumcircle, we can use the power of point \\(Q\\) with respect to the circumcircle. The power of point \\(Q\\) is given by:\n\\[ QA \\cdot QB = QY \\cdot QX. \\]\nSince \\(Q\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(B\\) to \\(AC\\), we can use the relationship in the circle. The power of point \\(Q\\) can also be written as:\n\\[ QA \\cdot QB = QX \\cdot QY. \\]\nWe know \\(QX = QY - XY\\). Since \\(XY\\) is a chord and we need to find the relationship with \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\), we use the fact that \\(AB \\cdot AC\\) can be found using the power of point and the segments given.\n\nGiven \\(PQ = 25\\) and \\(XP = 10\\), we can use the relationship in the right triangle formed by \\(P\\) and the tangents from \\(Q\\) to the circle. The power of point \\(Q\\) gives us:\n\\[ QA \\cdot QB = QY \\cdot QX = 25^2 = 625. \\]\nSince \\(QY = 15\\), we have \\(QX = 625 - 15 \\cdot 2 = 625 - 30 = 595\\), but we need to correct this to the actual segments on the circle.\n\nUsing the relationship \\(AB \\cdot AC = PQ^2 = 625\\), we get:\n\\[ AB \\cdot AC = 625. \\]\nThis can be written in the form \\(m\\sqrt{n}\\) where \\(m = 25\\) and \\(n = 1\\). Therefore, \\(m + n = 25 + 1 = 26\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{26}\n\\]",
