      "text": "We start by noting the given information in triangle \\(ABC\\). Point \\(P\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(C\\) to \\(AB\\), and point \\(Q\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(B\\) to \\(AC\\). The line \\(PQ\\) intersects the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) at points \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\). We are given that \\(XP = 10\\), \\(PQ = 25\\), and \\(QY = 15\\).\n\nFirst, we recognize that since \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(C\\) and \\(B\\) respectively to \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\), \\(PQ\\) is a symmetric line with respect to the altitude from \\(A\\) to \\(BC\\). This means that \\(PQ\\) is parallel to \\(BC\\) and the distances from \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) to \\(BC\\) are equal, but in opposite directions. Therefore, \\(PQ\\) is a line that is the radical axis of the circles with diameters \\(APC\\) and \\(BQC\\).\n\nSince \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) and \\(PQ\\) is a chord of the circle, we can use the Power of a Point theorem. The Power of a Point theorem states that for a point \\(P\\) outside a circle and a line through \\(P\\) that intersects the circle at points \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\), the product of the lengths of the segments from \\(P\\) to the points of intersection is equal to the power of the point with respect to the circle. In this case, the power of point \\(P\\) with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) is given by \\(PQ^2 = 25^2 = 625\\).\n\nThe power of point \\(P\\) is also equal to \\(PX \\cdot PY = PQ \\cdot PQ = 25 \\cdot 25 = 625\\). Therefore, \\(PX \\cdot PY = 625\\). Since \\(XP = 10\\), we can find \\(PY\\) by solving the equation:\n\\[\n10 \\cdot PY = 625 \\implies PY = \\frac{625}{10} = 62.5.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the product \\(AB \\cdot AC\\). Using the Power of a Point theorem again, we have:\n\\[\nXP \\cdot PY = \\(PQ \\cdot QY = 25 \\cdot 15 = 375.\n\\]\nWe can also express \\(AB \\cdot AC\\) in terms of the sides of the triangle and the altitude from \\(A\\) to \\(BC\\). The length of \\(PQ\\) is the altitude from \\(C\\) to \\(AB\\) times the sine of the angle \\(C\\), and the length \\(AB \\cdot AC\\) can be expressed as:\n\\[\nAB \\cdot AC = (BC \\cdot \\sin C) \\cdot (AC \\cdot \\sin B) = (AB \\cdot AC) \\cdot \\sin C \\cdot \\sin B.\n\\]\nSince the power of point \\(P\\) is 625, and we have already calculated \\(PQ = 25\\), we can use the fact that the product of the segments is 625. Therefore, the value of \\(AB \\cdot AC\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{216}.\n\\]",
